   195|            <!-- Section 1 -->
   196|            <section id="visa-types">
   197|                <h2>1. Visa Types for Medical Travel</h2>
   198|
   199|                <p>China offers several visa categories relevant to medical tourists. The right choice depends on your nationality, the length of your planned stay, and whether you have a medical invitation from a recognized institution.</p>
   200|
   201|                <div class="visa-card">
   202|                    <span class="visa-type visa-type-medical">Recommended</span>
   203|                    <h3>F Visa (Visit Visa) — for Medical Treatment</h3>
   204|                    <p>The <strong>F visa</strong> is China's official visa category for non-commercial exchange visits, including medical treatment. It is the most appropriate visa for international patients seeking medical services in China.</p>
   205|                    <ul>
   206|                        <li><strong>Duration:</strong> Typically single-entry, valid for 3-6 months</li>
   207|                        <li><strong>Stay permitted:</strong> Up to 90 days per entry (extendable inside China through the exit-entry bureau)</li>
   208|                        <li><strong>Requirements:</strong> Official invitation letter from a Chinese medical institution</li>
   209|                        <li><strong>Processing time:</strong> 4-10 business days at most Chinese embassies/consulates</li>
   210|                    </ul>
   211|                </div>
   212|
   213|                <div class="visa-card">
   214|                    <span class="visa-type visa-type-tourist">Also Available</span>
   215|                    <h3>L Visa (Tourist Visa)</h3>
   216|                    <p>For short medical visits (under 15 days for most nationalities), a tourist visa (L visa) may be sufficient, particularly if you have a simple outpatient procedure planned.</p>
   217|                    <ul>
   218|                        <li><strong>Duration:</strong> Single or double entry, valid for 3-6 months</li>
   219|                        <li><strong>Stay permitted:</strong> Typically 15-30 days per entry (depending on your passport)</li>
   220|                        <li><strong>Requirements:</strong> Hotel booking, itinerary, sometimes invitation letter</li>
   221|                        <li><strong>Limitation:</strong> Not ideal for major procedures requiring longer recovery time; extensions in China are complicated</li>
   222|                    </ul>
   223|                </div>
   224|
   225|                <div class="info-box">
   226|                    <h4>&#x1F4A1; Visa-Free Entry (2026)</h4>
   227|                    <p>As of 2026, China has expanded its visa-free transit programs significantly. Citizens of many countries (including many European, Asian, and American passports) can enter China visa-free for short stays (typically 72-144 hours) via designated ports. However, <strong>this does not cover medical treatment</strong> — you still need an F or M visa for medical purposes, even if you can enter visa-free for tourism purposes initially.</p>
   228|                </div>
   229|
   230|                <table class="comparison-table">
   231|                    <thead>
   232|                        <tr><th>Criteria</th><th>F Visa (Medical)</th><th>L Visa (Tourist)</th></tr>
   233|                    </thead>
   234|                    <tbody>
   235|                        <tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Medical treatment, medical exchange</td><td>Tourism, visiting friends</td></tr>
   236|                        <tr><td>Invitation required</td><td>Yes (hospital letter)</td><td>Sometimes</td></tr>
   237|                        <tr><td>Max stay per entry</td><td>Up to 90 days</td><td>Typically 15-30 days</td></tr>
   238|                        <tr><td>Extension in China</td><td>Possible via exit-entry bureau</td><td>Difficult</td></tr>
   239|                        <tr><td>Best for</td><td>Surgery, IVF, major treatments</td><td>Short consultations, minor procedures</td></tr>
   240|                    </tbody>
   241|                </table>
   242|            </section>
   243|
   244|            <!-- Section 2 -->
   245|            <section id="visa-letter">
   246|                <h2>2. The Visa Invitation Letter</h2>
   247|
   248|                <p>The <strong>visa invitation letter (VOI)</strong> is the most critical document for obtaining your F visa. It is issued by the Chinese hospital's International Medical Center and is an official document addressed to the Chinese embassy/consulate.</p>
   249|
   250|                <div class="info-box success">
   251|                    <h4>&#x2705; We Coordinate Invitation Letters</h4>
   252|                    <p>When you book through China Hospitals Guide, our coordinators at partner hospitals will issue the official invitation letter on your behalf. You do not need to arrange this separately — it's part of our coordination service. The letter typically takes 3-5 business days to process after you provide your passport details.</p>
   253|                </div>
   254|
   255|                <h3>What the Invitation Letter Contains</h3>
   256|                <p>The official visa invitation letter for medical travel typically includes:</p>
   257|                <ul>
   258|                    <li><strong>Your full name, nationality, date of birth, and passport number</strong></li>
   259|                    <li><strong>Hospital name and address</strong></li>
   260|                    <li><strong>Name of treating physician</strong></li>
   261|                    <li><strong>Planned treatment/procedure</strong></li>
   262|                    <li><strong>Planned dates of treatment and estimated length of stay</strong></li>
   263|                    <li><strong>Hospital's official stamp and a contact person</strong></li>
   264|                    <li><strong>Financial responsibility statement</strong> (confirming who pays for treatment)</li>
   265|                </ul>
   266|
   267|                <h3>How to Get the Invitation Letter</h3>
   268|                <div class="step-box">
   269|                    <div class="step-header">
   270|                        <div class="step-num">1</div>
   271|                        <div class="step-title">Confirm your hospital appointment</div>
   272|                    </div>
   273|                    <p>Once your appointment is booked with a partner hospital, the International Medical Center can issue the invitation letter.</p>
   274|                </div>
   275|                <div class="step-box">
   276|                    <div class="step-header">
   277|                        <div class="step-num">2</div>
   278|                        <div class="step-title">Submit passport details</div>
   279|                    </div>
   280|                    <p>Provide: full name (as in passport), nationality, passport number, date of birth, passport issue/expiry dates.</p>
   281|                </div>
   282|                <div class="step-box">
   283|                    <div class="step-header">
   284|                        <div class="step-num">3</div>
   285|                        <div class="step-title">Receive the invitation letter</div>
   286|                    </div>
   287|                    <p>The hospital sends the original invitation letter by courier (3-5 business days). Some hospitals can send PDF copies via email for faster processing — check with your coordinator.</p>
   288|                </div>
   289|                <div class="step-box">
   290|                    <div class="step-header">
   291|                        <div class="step-num">4</div>
   292|                        <div class="step-title">Submit visa application with the invitation letter</div>
   293|                    </div>
   294|                    <p>Include the invitation letter, your passport, completed visa application form, photo, and fee at your local Chinese embassy/consulate.</p>
   295|                </div>
   296|            </section>
   297|
   298|            <!-- Section 3 -->
   299|            <section id="visa-process">
   300|                <h2>3. Visa Application Process</h2>
   301|
   302|                <h3>Step-by-Step F Visa Application</h3>
   303|                <div class="timeline">
   304|                    <div class="timeline-item">
   305|                        <h4>Step 1: Complete the online visa application form</h4>
   306|                        <p>Go to the Chinese visa application website (cova.mvisaonline.com) and complete Form COVA. Print the confirmation page. Note: unlike the USESTA or Schengen visa, there is no fee calculation — simply complete the form and book an appointment.</p>
   307|                    </div>
   308|                    <div class="timeline-item">
   309|                        <h4>Step 2: Gather your documents</h4>
   310|                        <p>Passport (valid 6+ months), photo (48mm x 33mm, white background), visa confirmation page, invitation letter, proof of travel/accommodation.</p>
   311|                    </div>
   312|                    <div class="timeline-item">
   313|                        <h4>Step 3: Book an appointment</h4>
   314|                        <p>Book an appointment at your nearest Chinese embassy/consulate visa application center (CVASC). Processing times vary by location — check the specific consulate's website.</p>
   315|                    </div>
   316|                    <div class="timeline-item">
   317|                        <h4>Step 4: Submit application and pay fee</h4>
   318|                        <p>Attend your appointment, submit documents, provide biometrics (fingerprints). Pay the visa fee (varies by nationality and processing speed — typically $30-200 USD).</p>
   319|                    </div>
   320|                    <div class="timeline-item">
   321|                        <h4>Step 5: Collect your passport with visa</h4>
   322|                        <p>Standard processing: 4-10 business days. Rush processing available at most consulates for additional fee.</p>
   323|                    </div>
   324|                </div>
   325|
   326|                <div class="info-box warning">
   327|                    <h4>&#x26A0; US Citizens: Note Special Requirements</h4>
   328|                    <p>Chinese visa requirements for US citizens have changed recently. US passport holders may face additional documentation requirements. Check with your local Chinese embassy or use a visa service agency for the most current requirements before applying.</p>
   329|                </div>
   330|            </section>
   331|
   332|            <!-- Section 4 -->
   333|            <section id="travel">
   334|                <h2>4. Travel Planning</h2>
   335|
   336|                <h3>Major International Airports in China</h3>
   337|                <p>Most international patients fly into one of these gateway cities with direct international connections:</p>
   338|                <ul>
   339|                    <li><strong>Beijing Capital International Airport (PEK/PKX)</strong> — Best for patients going to Beijing hospitals; largest international hub in China</li>
   340|                    <li><strong>Shanghai Pudong International Airport (PVG)</strong> — Best for Shanghai hospitals; major international hub with excellent connections</li>
   341|                    <li><strong>Guangzhou Baiyun International Airport (CAN)</strong> — Best for Sun Yat-sen Cancer Center and other Guangdong hospitals</li>
   342|                    <li><strong>Hong Kong International Airport (HKG)</strong> — Good alternative with easy connection to Shenzhen hospitals via train/bus; some nationalities benefit from Hong Kong's simpler visa transit rules</li>
   343|                </ul>
   344|
   345|                <h3>Accommodation Near Partner Hospitals</h3>
   346|                <p>Most major hospitals are located in central urban areas with extensive hotel options nearby. For international patients, we recommend:</p>
   347|                <ul>
   348|                    <li><strong>Serviced apartments</strong> — Best for patients staying 1+ weeks; full kitchen, laundry, more space</li>
   349|                    <li><strong>Business hotels</strong> — Western-chain hotels (Marriott, Hilton, Hyatt) near hospital districts offer reliable quality and English-speaking staff</li>
   350|                    <li><strong>Boutique hotels</strong> — Often more affordable near major hospital areas</li>
   351|                </ul>
   352|                <p>Your coordinator can recommend accommodation options near your specific hospital.</p>
   353|
   354|                <h3>Local Transportation in China</h3>
   355|                <ul>
   356|                    <li><strong>Taxi/ride-hailing:</strong> Use <strong>Didi</strong> (China's Uber — works with international phone numbers) or regular taxis. Taxis in major cities are very affordable.</li>
   357|                    <li><strong>Metro:</strong> Beijing, Shanghai, Guangzhou, and other cities have extensive metro systems. Use Google Maps or Amap (高德地图) for navigation.</li>
   358|                    <li><strong>Train:</strong> For travel between cities, China's high-speed rail (G/D trains) is fast, comfortable, and affordable. Book via the 12306 app or at station ticket counters.</li>
   359|                </ul>
   360|
   361|                <div class="info-box">
   362|                    <h4>&#x1F4A1; Payment Apps You Need</h4>
   363|                    <p>China is largely a <strong>cashless society</strong>. You will need:</p>
   364|                    <ul style="margin-bottom: 0;">
   365|                        <li><strong>WeChat Pay or Alipay</strong> — Linked to a Chinese bank account for most payments. International versions (WeChat Pay HK, Alipay+) work for some tourists but may not work at hospitals. Ask your coordinator about payment options.</li>
   366|                        <li><strong>International credit cards</strong> — Accepted at most international hotels and some hospitals, but not universally.</li>
   367|                        <li><strong>Cash (CNY)</strong> — Keep some cash for smaller hospitals and local purchases.</li>
   368|                    </ul>
   369|                </div>
   370|            </section>
   371|
   372|            <!-- Section 5 -->
   373|            <section id="checklist">
   374|                <h2>5. Travel Documents Checklist</h2>
   375|
   376|                <div class="checklist-box">
   377|                    <h3>&#x1F9F9; Pre-Travel Documents Checklist</h3>
   378|                    <div class="checklist-item">
   379|                        <span class="check-icon">&#x2713;</span>
   380|                        <span class="check-text"><strong>Passport</strong> — Valid for 6+ months beyond your planned return date, at least 2 blank visa pages</span>
   381|                    </div>
   382|                    <div class="checklist-item">
   383|                        <span class="check-icon">&#x2713;</span>
   384|                        <span class="check-text"><strong>F Visa (in passport)</strong> — Obtained from Chinese embassy/consulate with hospital invitation letter</span>
   385|                    </div>
   386|                    <div class="checklist-item">
   387|                        <span class="check-icon">&#x2713;</span>
   388|                        <span class="check-text"><strong>Visa invitation letter</strong> — Original or printed copy from the hospital</span>
   389|                    </div>
   390|                    <div class="checklist-item">
   391|                        <span class="check-icon">&#x2713;</span>
   392|                        <span class="check-text"><strong>Medical records in English AND Chinese</strong> — Translated and printed; imaging CDs if applicable</span>
   393|                    </div>
   394|                    <div class="checklist-item">
   395|                        <span class="check-icon">&#x2713;</span>
   396|                        <span class="check-text"><strong>Flight itinerary</strong> — Confirmation of round-trip flights in and out of China</span>
   397|                    </div>
   398|                    <div class="checklist-item">
   399|                        <span class="check-icon">&#x2713;</span>
   400|                        <span class="check-text"><strong>Hotel confirmation</strong> — Booking near the hospital for your entire stay</span>
   401|                    </div>
   402|                    <div class="checklist-item">
   403|                        <span class="check-icon">&#x2713;</span>
   404|                        <span class="check-text"><strong>Travel insurance policy</strong> — Including medical evacuation and medical coverage; print the policy document and emergency contact numbers</span>
   405|                    </div>
   406|                    <div class="checklist-item">
   407|                        <span class="check-icon">&#x2713;</span>
   408|                        <span class="check-text"><strong>Emergency contact information</strong> — Your coordinator's phone/WeChat, hospital address in Chinese characters</span>
   409|                    </div>
   410|                    <div class="checklist-item">
   411|                        <span class="check-icon">&#x2713;</span>
   412|                        <span class="check-text"><strong>Vaccination certificate</strong> — If required by current regulations for your country of origin</span>
   413|                    </div>
   414|                    <div class="checklist-item">
   415|                        <span class="check-icon">&#x2713;</span>
   416|                        <span class="check-text"><strong>Chinese characters card</strong> — A card with your hospital's name and address written in Chinese characters to show a taxi driver</span>
   417|                    </div>
   418|                </div>
   419|            </section>
   420|
   421|            <!-- Summary -->
   422|            <section id="summary">
   423|                <h2>&#x1F4DD; Chapter Summary</h2>
   424|                <div class="info-box success">
   425|                    <h4>&#x2705; Key Takeaways</h4>
   426|                    <ol style="margin: 10px 0 0 20px;">
   427|                        <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;"><strong>Use the F visa for medical treatment</strong> — It allows up to 90 days stay and is the correct visa for medical tourism</li>
   428|                        <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;"><strong>The hospital invitation letter is essential</strong> — It is your key supporting document for the F visa application</li>
   429|                    </ol>
