                <h2>📝 Chapter Summary</h2>
                <div class="info-box success">
                    <h4>✅ Key Takeaways</h4>
                    <ol style="margin: 10px 0 0 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;"><strong>Language barriers are a patient safety issue, not just an inconvenience.</strong> Always use qualified medical interpretation for consultations, procedures, and informed consent — never rely on Google Translate or bilingual hospital staff who are not trained interpreters.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;"><strong>Understand the difference between services:</strong> Hospital international departments handle administration; medical interpreters handle communication; patient coordinators provide both interpretation and full advocacy.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;"><strong>Budget separately for language services.</strong> Interpretation and coordination are not included in hospital fees. Get written fee agreements before committing.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;"><strong>Ask the right questions.</strong> Verify credentials, experience with your specific hospital, and get references before hiring any coordinator.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;"><strong>Match service level to your needs.</strong> A simple consultation may only need remote interpretation; major surgery requires full coordination.</li>
                    </ol>
